.page echo 1 write its arguments to standard output

# SYNOPSIS
*echo* [*-neE*] [_STRING_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`echo` writes its arguments to standard output, separated by single spaces and followed by a newline. gapsh has a builtin `echo` that behaves the same way; this page describes both.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n*
    Do not write the trailing newline.
@ *-e*
    Interpret backslash escapes in the arguments (see below).
@ *-E*
    Do not interpret backslash escapes. This is the default.

The options may be combined (`-ne`) and must come first; the first argument that is not made only of the letters n, e and E ends them.

With `-e` these escapes are recognised:

    \a  alert (bell)           \b  backspace        \e  escape (ESC)
    \f  form feed              \n  newline          \r  carriage return
    \t  horizontal tab         \v  vertical tab     \\  a backslash
    \0NNN  the byte with octal value NNN (up to three digits)
    \c  stop here: write nothing more, not even the newline

# EXAMPLES
    echo hello gensokyo
    echo -n "no newline"
    echo -e 'one\ttwo\nthree'
    echo -e '\e[1;31mred\e[0m'

# EXIT STATUS
Always 0.

# SEE ALSO
printf(1), cat(1), gapsh(1)
